A Note on Text Encoding and the B.O.M.
--------------------------------------

Note that many files in the MyMan distribution are distributed as
Unicode/UCS text in the UTF-8 encoding with an explicit "byte-order
mark" (B.O.M.) at the beginning. This three-byte sequence (0xEF, 0xBB,
0xBF in hexadecimal notation) allows many web browsers and text
editors on Windows, Mac OS X, and other operating systems to
automatically select the correct character encoding when displaying or
editing the files. Please try to preserve this sequence when further
redistributing the files or sending in patches so that others will be
able to read the files painlessly.


Command-line Arguments, MYMANRC and the Environment
---------------------------------------------------

MyMan allows command-line arguments to be given in files and in an
environment variable. This allows frequently-used options to be stored
to save repetetive typing.

MyMan reads and recursively expands (in depth-first order) the
contents of the following according to the argument expansion file
syntax:

1. The $MYMANOPTS variable, if present. This text is parsed
indentically to the contents of an argument expansion file (even
comments are allowed.)

2. The start-up file (MYMANRC), one of:

2.1. The file $MYMANRC (if set, and if it points to a readable
file); or

2.2. The file ~/.mymanrc (if $HOME is not set "." is used
as a fallback); or

2.3. The file mymanrc.

See below for file search rules.

3. The contents of any words in the resulting command line.

File Search Rules
-----------------

Files with relative paths (including mymanrc) are searched for
first in the current directory, and then in $MYMANDIR
(PRIVATEDATADIR is used as a fallback if defined at compilation
time).

When looking for .mymanrc, _mymanrc is allowed as a fallback.

When looking for a filename not ending in .txt, .txt is
appended and tried as a fallback.

Argument Expansion Tokens
-------------------------

Command-line arguments (including those from $MYMANOPTS, MYMANRC and
argument expansion files) can cause expansion when they take the
following forms:

@@foo -> "@foo"
@ -> "@"
@file -> expanded words from file
@~/file -> expanded words from "$HOME/file" or "./file"
@- -> expanded words from stdin

Argument Expansion File Syntax
------------------------------

NUL is ignored.

CR LF, LF CR, and CR are each treated as a single LF.

QUOTE here represents a literal double-quote.

EOF represents end of file or end of environment variable string.

# ... -> comment to end of line or file

foo#bar -> foo#bar (as a single arg)

foo bar -> foo, bar (as separate args)

QUOTE foo bar QUOTE -> foo bar (as a single arg)

QUOTE foo

bar QUOTE -> foo LF bar (as a single arg)

QUOTE foo bar EOF -> foo bar (as a single arg)

foo QUOTE EOF -> foo QUOTE (as a single arg)

foo QUOTE bar baz QUOTE baq -> foobar bazbaq (as a single arg)

QUOTE foo QUOTE QUOTE bar QUOTE -> foo QUOTE bar (as a single arg)

QUOTE QUOTE foo bar QUOTE QUOTE baz QUOTE QUOTE baq -> QUOTE foo, bar
QUOTE, baz QUOTE baq as separate args

whitespace, LF -> ignored (works as a delimiter, though)

NOTE: Argument file expansion is depth-first and only a total
of $MYMAN_MAXINCLUDE argument expansion files are allowed
(default: 12). Once this limit is reached, any further
expansion attempts cause an error.

Environment Variables
---------------------

Some configurations of MyMan are influenced by the values of
environment variables, in addition to those behaviors documented for
the underlying display libraries:


